      "body": "## Motivation\n\n`RequestStage.DECODE_QUICK_FINISH` is declared with `metrics_is_observed=True`,\nbut nothing reports it, so the `quick_finish` span never appears in a trace and\nits per-stage latency is never observed. The identifier occurs exactly once in\nthe tree — at its own declaration.\n\n`set_quick_finish_time()` only stamped timestamps:\n\n```python\ndef set_quick_finish_time(self, ts=None):\n    ts = ts or time.perf_counter()\n    self.set_completion_time(ts)\n    self.forward_entry_time = ts\n```\n\nIts neighbours stamp *and* report. For example `set_decode_prebuilt_finish_time()`,\ncalled on the line immediately before it in `process_batch_result_prebuilt()`:\n\n```python\ndef set_decode_prebuilt_finish_time(self, ts=None):\n    ts = ts or time.perf_counter()\n    self.decode_prebuilt_finish_time = ts\n\n    stage = RequestStage.DECODE_FAKE_OUTPUT\n    self.observe_per_stage_req_latency(stage, ts - self.last_forward_entry_time)\n    self.trace_slice(stage, self.last_forward_entry_time, ts)\n```\n\nOf the 13 setters that only stamp, 12 are start/end pairs whose end half reports\nthe stage — `set_spec_draft_start_time` with `set_spec_draft_end_time`, and so on.\n`set_quick_finish_time()` has no end half, so its stage has no reporter at all.\n\n## Ordering\n\nThe report has to come **before** `set_completion_time()`. That call does:\n\n```python\nif self.trace_ctx.tracing_enable:\n    self.trace_ctx.abort()\n```\n\nand `abort()` ends with `self.thread_context = None`, after which\n`__check_fast_return()` returns True and `trace_slice()` returns at its first\nline. A slice appended after `set_completion_time()` would read correctly in the\ndiff and emit nothing at runtime.\n\n## Modifications\n\nReport the stage the way the neighbouring setters do, before the completion call,\nreusing `last_forward_entry_time` as the slice start so the timeline stays\nconsistent with `DECODE_FAKE_OUTPUT` recorded just before it.\n\n## Accuracy Test\n\nTwo tests in `test/registered/unit/observability/test_req_time_stats.py`:\n\n- `test_quick_finish_reports_its_stage` — the collector receives\n  `quick_finish` with the expected latency, and `trace_slice()` is called with\n  the stage and both timestamps. Fails on `main`, where neither happens.\n- `test_stage_is_reported_before_the_context_is_aborted` — records the call\n  order and asserts the slice precedes `set_completion_time()`. This is the part\n  that would silently regress if the report were ever moved after the abort.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Code Formatting with Pre-Commit](https://docs.sglang.ai/references/contribution_guide.html#code-formatting-with-pre-commit).\n- [x] Add unit tests as outlined in the [Running Unit Tests](https://docs.sglang.ai/references/contribution_guide.html#running-unit-tests-adding-to-ci).\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #35504419877](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/35504419877)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #35504419709](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/35504419709)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #35504419851](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/35504419851)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
